{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall not eat leavened bread with it for seven days.</b> Even though for the Pesach of Egypt the prohibition of leaven was only one day, as our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned (Pesachim 96a)." "<b>You shall eat matzot with it.</b> Since it is a sacrifice of remembrance of the redemption, which took place in a brief instant, it is not fitting to eat with it leaven, which requires delay in its preparation, from kneading the dough until it leavens (Hosea 7:4)." "<b>Bread of affliction.</b> Bread that they ate in affliction, when they had no leisure to let their dough remain until it leavened, because of urgent taskmasters." "<b>For in haste you went out.</b> And the reason for mentioning the haste of the bread is that in exchange for that haste of affliction, you afterward had the haste of redemption, as it says: וְהָפַכְתִּי אֶבְלָם לְשָׂשׂוֹן (Jeremiah 31:12)." "<b>To the end that you remember the day of your going out from the land of Egypt all the days of your life.</b> And the reason that I commanded to maintain the reckoning until the lunar years are aligned with the solar years through intercalation, so that Nisan be the month of spring — and I did not command to reckon by solar months, which require no intercalation — is so that you remember the exodus from Egypt all the days of your life: for with every intercalation of a month and intercalation of a year that is performed so that Nisan be the month of spring, you will remember that this is for the sake of the exodus from Egypt."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:21:34.412323180Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}